Your project targets **net10.0**, so here's the recommended approach for each category of static I found in `ReportGenerator.cs`:

---

### File System (`File.*`, `Directory.*`) → **System.IO.Abstractions**

Use the battle-tested NuGet package instead of a custom wrapper:

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

Register in DI:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

Inject into your class:
```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = ...;
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

Test with `MockFileSystem`:
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.AddDirectory("/tmp/reports");
var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);

var path = generator.GenerateReport("sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(path));
Assert.Contains("sales", mockFs.File.ReadAllText(path));
```

---

### Other statics I noticed in `ReportGenerator.cs`

| Static | Recommended Abstraction |
|---|---|
| `DateTime.UtcNow` | **`TimeProvider`** (built-in on .NET 10) — register `TimeProvider.System` in DI, test with `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` |
| `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable`, `Environment.MachineName` | Custom **`IEnvironmentProvider`** wrapper (only wrap the two members you actually call) |
| `Console.WriteLine` | Custom **`IConsole`** wrapper, or use `ILogger<T>` if this is really logging |

The file system statics you asked about are the easiest win — `System.IO.Abstractions` gives you a drop-in `IFileSystem` with a full mock implementation out of the box. Want me to apply these changes to `ReportGenerator.cs`?
